voprf — provenance note
========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`frost`/`musig2`/`adaptor` modules — self-contained crypto modules not
folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

voprf — clean-room implementation from RFC 9497 ("Oblivious
Pseudorandom Functions (OPRFs) Using Prime-Order Groups", IRTF CFRG,
December 2023), **ristretto255-SHA512 ciphersuite (§4.1) only**,
covering: the prime-order-group interface (§2.1), the batched DLEQ
proof system — `GenerateProof`/`ComputeCompositesFast` (§2.2.1) and
`VerifyProof`/`ComputeComposites` (§2.2.2) — the protocol configuration
and `CreateContextString` (§3.1), key generation including
`DeriveKeyPair` (§3.2/§3.2.1), and the online protocols for all three
modes: OPRF `Blind`/`BlindEvaluate`/`Finalize`/`Evaluate` (§3.3.1),
VOPRF `BlindEvaluate`/`Finalize` (§3.3.2), and POPRF
`Blind`/`BlindEvaluate`/`Finalize`/`Evaluate` (§3.3.3). RFC 9497 is a
public IRTF/IETF specification — not a copyrightable work (merger
doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no third-party-source NOTICE
entry is strictly required for the spec citation alone. No reference
implementation's source was ported or consulted as a design reference —
this pass worked from the RFC 9497 text
(https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9497.txt) and the public API of
`std.crypto.ecc.Ristretto255` only (no `cloudflare/voprf-ts`, no
`bytemare/oprf`, no other OPRF implementation consulted for algorithm
shape or code).

`HashToGroup`/`HashToScalar`'s `expand_message_xmd` construction
additionally follows RFC 9380 ("Hashing to Elliptic Curves", §5.4.1
expand_message_xmd and Appendix B hash_to_ristretto255) — likewise a
public IETF specification, not a copyrightable work, no reference
implementation consulted; `expandMessageXmd` (`root.zig`) is a
clean-room transcription of RFC 9380's own pseudocode, specialized to
SHA-512 / `len_in_bytes <= 64` (`ell = 1`, this ciphersuite's only
case), and cross-validated (`kat_test.zig`) against RFC 9380's own
Appendix K.3 published vectors.

The RFC 9497 Appendix A.1 "ristretto255-SHA512" test vectors
(`kat_vectors.zig` — OPRF A.1.1, VOPRF A.1.2, POPRF A.1.3, including
the batch-size-2 vectors) were transcribed directly from
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9497.txt for this pass. These are
public specification artifacts — the RFC's own published conformance
vectors — not copied from any other implementation's test suite. Only
the ristretto255-SHA512 vectors (of the five ciphersuites RFC 9497
Appendix A publishes — ristretto255, decaf448, P-256, P-384, P-521)
are embedded; see `root.zig`'s module doc comment and `SPEC.md` for why
the others are out of scope. The RFC 9380 Appendix K.3
`expand_message_xmd(SHA-512)` vectors embedded alongside them have the
same public-specification provenance.

`std.crypto.ecc.Ristretto255` (group arithmetic, `basePoint`, `mul`,
`add`/`sub`, `fromBytes`/`toBytes`, `fromUniform`, `rejectIdentity`),
its `scalar` field arithmetic (`reduce64`, `add`/`sub`/`mul`,
`rejectNonCanonical`, `Scalar.invert`), and `std.crypto.hash.sha2.
Sha512` (all MIT, part of Zig itself) are used via their public APIs;
no new curve or hash primitive is implemented here.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this
module.

**Status: complete.** All OPRF, VOPRF, and POPRF cores implemented and
KAT-validated byte-exact against RFC 9497 Appendix A.1 (19/19 tests,
Debug + ReleaseFast). See `SPEC.md` for the threat model and the DLEQ
construction notes.

Extension 2026-11-13: all five ciphersuites
-------------------------------------------

The decaf448-SHAKE256, P256-SHA256, P384-SHA384 and P521-SHA512 suites
(RFC 9497 §4.2-§4.5) were added from the same RFC text, with the groups
taken from this repository's sibling modules (`decaf448`, `p256`, `p384`,
`p521`) through their public APIs. `src/h2c.zig` (RFC 9380 §5.3
expand_message_xmd/xof, §5.2 hash_to_field, §6.6.2 simplified SWU,
Appendix F.2.1.2 sqrt_ratio) is a clean-room transcription of RFC 9380's
own pseudocode; no other hash-to-curve implementation was consulted. The
RFC 9497 Appendix A.1-A.5 vectors (`src/suite_vectors.zig`) and the RFC
9380 Appendix J.1.1/J.2.1/J.3.1 and K.1/K.3 vectors (`src/h2c_vectors.zig`)
are generated from https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9497.txt and
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9380.txt by `tools/gen_vectors.py`,
which only re-flows the RFCs' wrapped hex; they are public specification
artifacts.
